<header><p class="enc-kicker">Historical factor</p><h1 class="enc-title">Multi-ethnic integration</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Multi-ethnic integration is the recurring pattern by which empires incorporated diverse peoples into their armies, administrations and elites. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and Turks, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> employed Turkic and Sogdian commanders, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> governed many religious communities. Such policies broadened an empire's base of support, though tensions between groups could also contribute to decline.</p></div>
